8187324,"kt5":61.7321870594126,"kt6":26.247650878766454,"ly_tot":173.89064373600002,"ly5":52.783145597276445,"ly6":21.365626271090342},{"k":202710,"tot":167.4366041223172,"kt5":75.78360409191323,"kt6":37.39418971800976,"ly_tot":159.68622504000004,"ly5":54.032905329220576,"ly6":23.032913866917006}]}},"nowcast":{},"temps_since":[{"k":202611,"t":9.992373613727441,"ly":9.63511715702162}],"paragraphs":["<b>State of play, end of October 2026.</b> G2025 — the generation being harvested — has 116.2 M fish left against 130.7 M for G2024 a year earlier (−11.1 %), but they weigh 4.11 kg against 3.80 kg, so its biomass is −3.7 % and its stock of fish over 5 kg gutted +40 % (82 kt against 59). Over the last three months it grew 0.64 kg per fish a month against 0.56 (+14 %) in water at 12.0 °C against 13.0 °C — temperature-corrected (TGC) +18 % — and lost 0.97 % of its fish a month against 1.31 %. G2026, which takes over next year, has 315.1 M fish (+0.2 %) at 1.23 kg against 1.19 (biomass +4 %), growing +8 % faster per fish, TGC +10 %. Fish over 5 kg harvested in the last three months: 165 kt gutted against 139 (+18 %), out of 501 kt in all (+9 %).","<b>The gap between generations.</b> Until 2024, the big fish ran short every year while the harvest moved to the new generation: the lowest three months of 5 kg+ harvested between April and September fell to about 74–92 % of the October–March monthly average, and the market was sold small fish while waiting for the 5+/6+. Lowest point ÷ winter average, season by season: 2017-18 <b>86 %</b> · 2018-19 <b>89 %</b> · 2019-20 <b>85 %</b> · 2020-21 <b>76 %</b> <span style=\"color:var(--muted)\">(atypical)</span> · 2021-22 <b>68 %</b> <span style=\"color:var(--muted)\">(atypical)</span> · 2022-23 <b>74 %</b> · 2023-24 <b>92 %</b> · 2024-25 <b>144 %</b> · 2025-26 <b>99 %</b>. The last two seasons broke the pattern: the 5 kg+ held at 144 % and 99 % of the winter level (in 2024 the shortage of big fish came in the autumn instead, September–December, with only a fifth of the harvest over 5 kg), and the stock of 5 kg+ fish at sea never fell below 22 and 21 kt, against 9–14 kt in the years before: the new generations now reach harvest weight months earlier.","<b>Next transition (Apr-Jul 2027): no gap in sight.</b> Projected 5 kg+ harvest against the same months of 2026: <b>+15 %</b> if the new generation keeps most of its advance, +3 % if the advance fades out, +20 % if it keeps all of it; 6 kg+ +9 % to +30 %; all sizes −4 % to +8 %. Lowest point ÷ winter average in the season ahead: 90–97 % depending on the scenario, against 99 % this year. The risk is rather the other way: if the advance holds, a wave of 6 kg+ in spring–early summer, more than the market took this year. ","<b>Two-month assumptions.</b> November: 148 kt gutted (+10 % to +10 %), of which 5 kg+ about 61 kt (+24 % on a year earlier) and 6 kg+ 30 kt (+33 %); December: 120 kt gutted (−4 % to 0 %), of which 5 kg+ about 55 kt (+5 % on a year earlier) and 6 kg+ 27 kt (+5 %). Behind them: G2025 keeps being harvested at the pace usual for its weight (so faster than G2024, being heavier), its mortality stays at the last three months' level, and sea temperature follows the normal with today's anomaly fading. ","<b>Next months.</b> Nov 2026 – Jan 2027: all sizes +5 % to +5 % on a year earlier, 5 kg+ +16 % (central). Feb 2027 – Apr 2027: all sizes −4 % to +4 %, 5 kg+ +30 %. Sea temperature Nov 2026: 10.0 °C against 9.6.","<b>What the November register (due early January) will tell.</b> Fish left in G2025: about 87 M expected against 101 M for G2024 a year earlier (−14 %, from −11 % now); clearly fewer would mean the big fish are being pulled forward and the spring tail gets thinner, clearly more that harvesting is slowing down. The decisive figure is G2026's temperature-corrected growth: still +10 % on G2025 now; if it stays above about +10 %, read the 'advance kept' column (more 5–6 kg+ in spring, the risk becomes too many big fish); if it falls back to zero or below, read 'advance fades' (5 kg+ flat on this year, total volume a little down in the first half). Mortality of G2025 and G2026 (now 0.97 % and 1.25 % a month) moves the volume, not the timing.","<span style=\"color:var(--muted)\">How it is worked out: the same zone-by-generation simulation (growth = usual TGC at that weight and month × the generation's own recent advance × expected temperature; harvest = usual share of the stock taken at that weight and month; mortality = last three months) is run from this month and from the same month a year earlier, and the ratio of the two is applied to what was really harvested a year earlier, so most of the simulation's own errors cancel. Tested from September 2025: April–July 2026 5 kg+ forecast 206 kt, actual 215 kt; the winter was under-forecast (224 against 312 kt) because growth accelerated. 2021–2022 are left out of what is learned. Size classes come from average weights with a lognormal spread, so they are estimates. Rebuilt after every register.</span>"]}